WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.870
- It's funny because the
simulation hypothesis,

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:03.650
for the listeners that may not know,

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:05.260
is basically the supposition

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.160
that as computing power doubles
every year, year and a half,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.080
according to what's known as Moore's law,

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.680
your computers get cheaper,
they get more powerful,

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.660
they get faster, and
that that extrapolation,

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.110
there's no natural starting point,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.050
I mean, when you made the
first computer or whatever,

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:22.180
that for us is.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.200
But could it not be that
they get to so much power

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.350
that they can simulate
every single physical object

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.340
in the universe, and if
we're just material objects,

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.020
our brains are just wet computers,

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:35.960
but they're just computers,

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.060
they have neurons, they have
carbon, and DNA, and so forth,

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.840
but why couldn't that be simulated

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.970
in some great, vast
supercomputer of the future

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.460
that doesn't exist yet?

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.610
But we would be kind of like
the "SimCity" or whatever.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:51.900
- We'd be in "The Matrix," in essence.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.557
- Yeah, we'd be in "The Matrix."

00:00:53.557 --> 00:00:56.724
(gentle solemn music)

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.850
- I'm Dave Rubin and joining me today

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.040
is a distinguished professor of physics

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.580
at the Center for Astrophysics
and Space Sciences

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.077
at UC San Diego, and
author of the new book,

00:01:11.077 --> 00:01:15.150
"Into the Impossible: Think
Like a Nobel Prize Winner."

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.130
Brian Keating, finally,
welcome to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:18.963
- Thank you, Dave.

00:01:18.963 --> 00:01:20.860
You were my first big guest on my podcast,

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.530
so it's nice to return the favor.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:23.600
I know you've been looking forward

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.794
to having a big guest
like me on for a while.

00:01:25.794 --> 00:01:27.320
(both laughing)

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.700
- I've wanted a man of science
on for quite some time.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.820
No, I've wanted to talk to
you for a while on this show,

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.880
because I think this will
be a little bit different

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.270
than the shows that we normally do.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.140
But framing one thing sort of politically,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.910
just to jump us off,

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:46.010
as a man of science and as
a man that just wrote a book

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:50.000
about how scientists approach science,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.650
what do you make of the state
of science at the moment?

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:56.630
Science is getting pretty
banged up these days.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.210
- Yeah, it's tough.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.350
I used to say I enjoy being an astronomer

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.760
because there's no Republican asteroids,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.350
there's no Democratic
comets, it's apolitical.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.380
But it's become incredibly
politicized, as you point out.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.250
I always point out, Dave,
the word science in Latin,

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.720
I know you're a scholar
so you'll know this,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.060
the word science means knowledge.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.440
It doesn't mean wisdom.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.170
So to expect wisdom from people

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.280
that are knowledge junkies...

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.560
Wikipedia has a lot of knowledge.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.440
It doesn't have any wisdom.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.560
You wouldn't want to trust
your dog with it, right?

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.840
So I do believe that we're living

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.430
in a fundamentally
scientific technological age

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.710
that kind of is worshiping scientists

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.530
way more than I think we warrant.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.550
As anyone who's ever known the
famous joke about scientists,

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.460
how do you know a scientist is outgoing?

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.022
Because he looks at your
shoes when he talks to you.

00:02:45.022 --> 00:02:45.855
(both laughing)

00:02:45.855 --> 00:02:48.900
And it's part of the goal of this book

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.900
is to humanize scientists

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.560
and to recognize that
we're just human beings.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.240
We have no special truth claims

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.163
or wisdom claims than anyone else.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:02.240
- Does that chasm between
knowledge and wisdom

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.420
sorta capture exactly the
moment we're at in a way?

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.170
Where it's like, we hear
things every day in the news.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:11.060
Okay, there's a new study
that says something.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.990
That tells us something about
knowledge to some degree.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.730
But then the wisdom of the policies

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.100
that then we are told we have to follow

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.530
often seems a little screwy,
at least in my humble opinion.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:26.510
- Yeah, looking again, if you
look to somebody for wisdom,

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.790
again looking to Wikipedia for wisdom

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:31.140
is not gonna be too fruitful.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.020
And yet we crave it,

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.920
because we worship at
the altar of science,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.300
in some way justifiably so.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.930
It's led to life increases,

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.890
child mortality rates plummeting,
vaccines, and so forth

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.270
that are just miraculous

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.790
and any other way would be
difficult to describe it,

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:51.130
understanding the composition
evolution of the universe

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.230
to a fraction of a
percent, just astonishing.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:58.530
And yet we don't as
laity, as a lay person,

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.410
don't really have a good
understanding of how science works.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.047
So as the words of Arthur C. Clarke said,

00:04:03.047 --> 00:04:04.860
"Any sufficiently advanced technology

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.750
is indistinguishable from magic,"

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.250
and some say it could be an
indistinguishable from a God.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.870
And so I think there is a tendency,

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:14.070
a desire to worship at
the altar of science,

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.060
and I think that's misplaced.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.300
Because we don't elect
scientists as a population.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.180
We elect politicians, a
president, governors, et cetera.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.820
We elect those people
and they wanna pass off

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.750
I think some of their
responsibility to the scientists

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:26.660
who we don't elect

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.283
and we wouldn't elect
in any other capacity.

00:04:30.587 --> 00:04:32.070
- So last part on this specifically,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.070
but do you sense that
sort of the reputation

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:38.540
that science and scientists
have gotten over the last year

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.210
where it's been so
battered and inconsistent,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.090
and wear a mask, don't wear a mask,

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:44.710
the vax is working, it's not working,

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.380
booster shots, the whole thing,

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.180
do you think it can recover
in the next couple of years?

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.210
Because right now, when they
just trot out the next study,

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:53.900
like at this point, I'm just like,

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.110
this is just more nonsense.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.800
I'm not saying I don't believe
that the study was conducted,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.010
but what it will always
lead to is just like,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.940
I can't listen to this stuff anymore.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.640
- Well, the biggest misconception

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.690
is that science is ever settled.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.870
I think the public has
that to their dismay

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.710
because believing that science is settled

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:16.160
is antithetical to science.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.400
Think about it, Dave.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.127
If Galileo came along and said,

00:05:19.127 --> 00:05:21.020
"Well, Aristotle was a scientist.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.000
He's very smart.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.290
I'll just accept that there
are only four elements

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.240
instead of 114,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.940
or that heavy things fall
faster than light things."

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.770
All these things, he could
have verified by the way,

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:32.970
but he didn't actually go out and do that.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.560
But Galileo would never have come up

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.180
with the initial Laws of Relativity,

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.907
same with Newton, Einstein, because,

00:05:38.907 --> 00:05:41.500
"Well, Newton is a genius,
I can outdo Newton,"

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.310
as he often looked up to him.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.460
So therefore, as Richard Feynman,

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.877
the great Nobel laureate
of the 20th century said,

00:05:47.877 --> 00:05:51.590
"Science is the belief in
the ignorance of experts."

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:54.300
Otherwise, you'd have no motivation

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.470
to ever modify, refine, and improve upon

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.280
who or what came before you.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.230
So I view that and I
view it to the detriment

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.640
of science and scientists themselves.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.310
Often people say, "Oh, I'm no Einstein,

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:08.190
I can't understand it."

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.023
Well, guess what, Dave?

00:06:09.023 --> 00:06:11.620
Einstein wasn't Einstein in
the beginning of his life.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.980
So, I think the more
that we kind of venerate

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.840
without explaining how
science is actually done,

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.920
it harms our own practitioners
of the craft of science

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.960
that I am engaged in.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.340
- Yeah, and that really actually
is what the book is about

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:28.230
because you talk about
things like creativity

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.880
and human expression and passion,

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.120
all of these things that
we don't really associate

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.600
with scientists and
their work specifically.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:36.450
So, let's just dive in.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:37.610
So you have nine chapters here,

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.120
each one going off a different scientist

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:40.953
for a different reason.

00:06:40.953 --> 00:06:42.490
I thought we could just
spend a couple of minutes

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.070
on everybody, but you gotta
leave some secrets here,

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:46.360
otherwise people won't buy the book.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:48.790
So a couple of minutes on
everybody, I know how it works.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:52.060
Chapter one is about Adam
Riess, the Star Gazer.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:54.320
- Yeah, so Adam is a contemporary of mine.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.430
He's basically my age, and
back in I think it was 2005,

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:59.320
there was a worldwide competition

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:02.550
to determine who is the greatest scientist

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:03.830
under the age of 40.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:04.910
And he and I entered it.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:08.390
I came in first as the most
promising scientist of all time,

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:11.233
at least of that age, and
then Adam came in third.

00:07:12.358 --> 00:07:15.420
And so later, six years later
when he won the Nobel Prize,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.770
my older brother, Kevin,
who watches this show,

00:07:18.884 --> 00:07:19.757
he called to report on me and said,

00:07:19.757 --> 00:07:23.671
"Brian, you won the battle,
but Adam won the war, brother.

00:07:23.671 --> 00:07:24.810
(both laughing)

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.390
You had the temporary reprieve."

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:30.660
So Adam is an incredibly
curious individual.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.110
He's very young, and what is often said

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:34.420
about the Nobel prize,

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.630
in fact, by Nobel laureate, T.S. Eliot,

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:38.080
who won it for literature,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:41.560
he said, "The Nobel is a
ticket to your own funeral

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.670
because no one ever does
it after he wins it."

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.790
And Adam has a real
counterexample to that canard

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.610
because he has gone on to great heights,

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.590
astronomically and otherwise,

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.200
educational mentorship, et cetera.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.680
I think he best exemplifies the fact that,

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:00.930
Dave, I never thought I could
be a professional astronomer.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:04.487
Growing up as we did on
Long Island, I thought like,

00:08:04.487 --> 00:08:06.050
"Oh, someone's gonna pay
me to be an astronomer

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:08.720
like they're gonna pay me
to be an ice cream taster

00:08:09.737 --> 00:08:11.070
or a wizard or something like that."

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.290
So, I really do look at him

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.460
as the fulfillment of this
dream that you can do it.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:19.340
You can achieve your passion.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:20.670
But passion alone is not enough.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.020
You need to have insatiable curiosity.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.200
I say passion is a spark,

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.240
but the fuel is actually curiosity.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.300
- I have to tell you that
growing up on Long Island,

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.800
as you noted, in 1997,

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:34.730
I think I told you this
when I was on your podcast,

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.310
I ate some pot brownies

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:37.980
and then went to see the movie "Contact"

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.020
by the great science communicator,

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:41.000
Carl Sagan.
- Carl Sagan.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.730
- And I was basically tripping,
and I'm sure you've seen

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:45.240
the beginning of that movie many times,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.630
that spectacular panorama of the universe.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:50.930
And it blew my mind,
and I ended up reading,

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:52.000
I think I've read every one

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.910
of Carl Sagan's books since then.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.340
- Wow.

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:56.780
- He had passed away I think a few months

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:57.870
before the movie came out,

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.480
but I had read all of his books.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.950
So it is possible that
someone from Long Island

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.640
could stare into the
stars and think something.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.657
- Yeah, I had his wife,
his widow, Ann Druyan,

00:09:07.657 --> 00:09:09.650
who is a co-author on "Contact,"

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:12.420
and his daughter on my
podcast, Sasha Sagan,

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.800
and he had something very
pertinent to your book.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:15.860
You know, "Don't Burn This Book."

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:17.860
He said, "Books," Dave, "are magic."

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:21.530
They are the implantation of
an author, long dead perhaps,

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:22.800
although not for you or me

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.000
hopefully for many centuries to come,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.580
and the wisdom distilled, per word,

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.070
the price per word of wisdom in a book.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.960
Carl, you, hopefully me, these
are really invaluable things,

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:36.800
and I think of these as hacks.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.840
And these scholars that
I profile in the book,

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:39.920
they all make use of that

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.000
and credit these great
scientists and authors

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.460
that came before.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:45.293
- Let me ask you one other thing

00:09:45.293 --> 00:09:47.190
about the people that
look into the distance

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:48.360
that are always trying to figure out

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:49.193
what's going on in the cosmos

00:09:49.193 --> 00:09:50.620
and the stars and everything else,

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:53.720
you'll hear now, "Oh, but we
have so many problems here.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:55.550
We shouldn't be funding
any of these things."

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:56.650
And we know that a lot of it now,

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:58.510
a lot of the exploration
is being done privately,

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:00.840
obviously Elon Musk and some other people.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:04.130
What do you make of that general outlook?

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:08.080
- Well, I think that the
separating out the space program

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.720
and exploration of space
from pure research,

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:13.500
so what I do is called
in one of the chapters

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.450
by Nobel laureate, Sheldon Glashow,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:18.980
what I do, the origin evolution
of the universe, cosmology,

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:21.230
not to be confused with cosmetology,

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:23.830
that you can tell I'm a
master practitioner thereof,

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:29.750
that we do, in his words,
useless ideas. (chuckling)

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.390
We basically pursue stuff
that has no net benefit,

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:34.930
at least so we think now,
and here's a good example.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:37.280
The discovery of this emanation

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:38.720
from the Big Bang that I study

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.580
called the cosmic microwave
background radiation,

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:44.700
it's an all-pervasive glow
of radio or microwaves.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:47.130
It was discovered
completely serendipitously

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:51.080
by two radio astronomers in
of all places, New Jersey,

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.790
Holmdel, New Jersey, and
they couldn't get rid of it.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.310
But the reason that they were so vital

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:00.430
to even the communication
technology that we're having now,

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:02.160
they were working at Bell Laboratories.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:04.800
Now, Bell Laboratories
doesn't exist anymore,

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.710
at least in that form, and
that's much more detrimental,

