And in this case, I wanted
to kind of demystify,

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:57.460
how should we be teaching?

00:21:57.460 --> 00:21:58.660
Because I don't know if you know it,

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:01.530
but the way that we
teach with like some dude

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:04.450
scraping a piece of rock
on another piece of rock,

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:07.870
that is almost exactly a
thousand-year-old model.

00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:10.720
It's not been disrupted
since the year 1080

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:11.870
in Bologna, Italy,

00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:15.180
when the first modern university
incarnation came along,

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:16.673
where you'd have some guy.

00:22:18.123 --> 00:22:18.956
But the only thing that's better now

00:22:18.956 --> 00:22:22.110
is that back then, Dave, the
students could go on strike

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:24.160
and the professors would not get paid.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:25.560
So, thank God we have tenure.

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.620
I mean, yeah, that is barbaric.

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.080
That is a barbaric practice.

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:30.740
But other than that, what has changed?

00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:31.940
So I got into all sorts of things.

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:34.580
Could we have an artificial
intelligent professor?

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:37.140
Could we alleviate some of the burden,

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.157
but also free up the minds

00:22:39.157 --> 00:22:41.080
and the time of the student to do it.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:43.890
And he likened our current
practice of teaching

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:47.600
to the medical practices
of the 18 and 17th century

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.590
with leeches and bloodletting.

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:51.140
He said we're at a primitive state

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:52.730
and we need to go beyond that.

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:54.070
He has some models to do it.

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:55.810
I don't think any of them are foolproof,

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:57.770
but the key takeaway I took, Dave,

00:22:57.770 --> 00:23:00.350
is that to be a good teacher,

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:02.890
you have to be a student of teaching.

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:04.920
And I pointed out in the book

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:07.820
that the word scientist
in the Russian language

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:09.840
means someone who was taught.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.220
To me that conveys an obligation.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:13.730
You both have to be a good student,

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:15.210
but you have to be a good teacher

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:17.410
to pay it forward to the next generation.

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:19.800
And so I think he exemplifies that to me

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:21.500
and really made me wanna double down

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:24.930
and be a better teacher
by studying teaching.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.800
And that's not something I
really have that much time to do,

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:30.920
but I find a little bit of
effort goes a long way with it.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:33.700
And I hope in anybody's field,
the way to learn something,

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:36.190
to be a better salesman,
to be a better podcaster.

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:37.827
I think I asked you when
you came out, I was like,

00:23:37.827 --> 00:23:39.930
"How do you know Dave, when
you're giving a good interview,

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.215
when you're doing a good
job as an interviewer?"

00:23:42.215 --> 00:23:44.370
You don't have a grader anymore,

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:46.360
but you must study it and
you must listen to it,

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:47.810
and even if that means
studying with people

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:50.170
that maybe you don't
agree with all the time,

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:51.040
you can learn a lot.

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:53.400
And that's part of the humble nature

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:55.000
that I think it takes to be a good student

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:56.220
and, ergo, a good teacher.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:57.760
- It's interesting because
as you're talking about that,

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:59.630
I'm thinking of all of the videos

00:23:59.630 --> 00:24:01.060
that we see on Twitter everyday,

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:03.770
where a student will
take a video of a teacher

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:05.580
just doing everything wrong,

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:07.750
either the way they're
trying to brainwash them

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:09.170
or yell at them and whatever.

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:10.003
And it's just like, man,

00:24:10.003 --> 00:24:13.520
there is so much broken in our
education system altogether,

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:16.340
but that's for a different show.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:19.620
Roger Penrose, the Singular Mind.

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:23.180
- Ah, yes, so Roger just
celebrated his 90th birthday

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:25.440
and he made appearance in
"The Theory of Everything,"

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:28.950
the movie with Eddie Redmayne, I guess,

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:30.150
and that was about Stephen Hawking,

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:32.850
who is his famous late, great friend

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:36.370
and collaborator and
colleague at Cambridge

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:38.450
while Roger was at Oxford.

00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:41.730
So, Roger came up with really whimsical,

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:45.660
almost whimsical drawings
and cartoons and schematics

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:48.880
that demonstrate what would
happen to your watch, Dave,

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:51.050
as you get close to a black hole.

00:24:51.050 --> 00:24:53.070
What would happen as you go beyond

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:56.560
what's called the event horizon,
how would things proceed?

00:24:56.560 --> 00:24:57.840
How would your future look?

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.300
How would you look to
someone on the outside world?

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:02.250
And he did this very intuitively

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:04.400
through a series of cartoons almost.

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:06.530
And it's in his tradition as an artist,

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:08.270
he's really a mathematician,

00:25:08.270 --> 00:25:10.100
and he's invented these wonderful things

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:12.570
that are evocative of Escher,

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:15.700
drawings with staircases that
just seem to keep going up

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:19.080
or these fishes that tile this
whole sphere and so forth.

00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:22.510
And he thinks geometrically,
he thinks artistically.

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:23.620
His dad was an artist.

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:25.370
And many of these men, unfortunately,

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:28.480
I tried to get the two
living Nobel Prize laureates

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:30.790
in physics that are female.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:33.410
They both rejected me, so that
took me back to high school,

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:35.340
and getting rejected by brilliant women.

00:25:35.340 --> 00:25:36.630
(Dave laughing)

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:37.463
But for various reasons,

00:25:37.463 --> 00:25:39.208
they weren't able to do it.
- Interesting.

00:25:39.208 --> 00:25:40.760
- But I'm hoping hope springs
eternal and I'll keep going.

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:42.910
I hope there's more female
laureates, obviously.

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:44.130
I have daughters myself

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:45.920
and then maybe someday
they could be among them.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:50.400
But the point being, when I asked them,

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:54.460
the most common thing I got
was a father or a teacher.

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:55.550
And I took that very seriously.

00:25:55.550 --> 00:25:58.740
Again, that you can be a father,

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:00.760
even if you don't have
biological children.

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:03.860
These men are ideological
fathers in some sense

00:26:03.860 --> 00:26:05.860
to millions of people around the world.

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:08.390
And yet they haven't
written their biography.

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:10.150
I mean, none of them have a biography,

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:11.280
which is kind of interesting.

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:12.940
So I wanted to do that as a service

00:26:12.940 --> 00:26:15.470
such that maybe someone
could be influenced by them

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:16.700
the way that they've influenced me

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:18.070
through their technical writing.

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:20.130
But you might not know that
actually they're really good

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:24.640
with dealing with vengeful
competitors and things like that.

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:27.700
That is actually what we deal
with day-to-day, isn't it?

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:30.430
It's not stroking my non-existent beard,

00:26:30.430 --> 00:26:32.380
thinking about contemplating the cosmos.

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:34.830
I get to do that on
occasion, but not that often.

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:38.300
Mostly it's dealing with
troublesome suppliers and vendors

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:39.840
and getting equipment to the site

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:41.590
and the student's having a problem.

00:26:43.081 --> 00:26:43.914
I wanted to go beyond that.

00:26:43.914 --> 00:26:47.040
And I think having mentors
and avuncular figures

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:48.070
like I do in the book,

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:50.240
I think, I'm hoping that
that will influence people

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:51.920
to see the human side of science.

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:52.930
- Right, and also of course,

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:56.770
doing some of this
through art and technology

00:26:56.770 --> 00:26:58.170
can awaken people in a way

00:26:58.170 --> 00:27:00.080
that just the guy at
the chalkboard can't do.

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:02.210
How did "Interstellar" do in that regard

00:27:02.210 --> 00:27:04.660
when they were going over
that event horizon, right?

00:27:04.660 --> 00:27:07.310
Because they had to drop the robot.

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:10.010
I forget what he was called,
TARS had to be dropped in.

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:11.280
There's a lot of imagery there

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:14.080
about light can't escape
from the other side

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:15.010
and all of that stuff.

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:16.280
How'd they do, how'd they do?

00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:18.710
- So that was all influenced
by Sir Roger Penrose

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:19.950
who influenced Kip Thorne,

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:22.630
who is another laureate that
I hope to get on the show

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:24.820
who was responsible for
winning the Nobel Prize,

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:26.700
along with colleagues,
for the LIGO experiment,

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:28.400
Ray Weiss we already talked about.

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:33.010
You can take a lot of what
I call physicist license

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:35.030
because these things are not approachable.

00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:36.090
It's not like...

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:39.140
I was talking on a
podcast earlier this week

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:41.330
with a biology professor
and she was talking about,

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:43.710
and I was like, "Don't
talk to me about that

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:46.570
because I'm so bad at biology

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:48.780
that when I dissected
a frog in high school,

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:50.047
the frog lived."

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:52.290
I just couldn't do it.

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:53.860
But in astronomy, so in other words,

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:55.010
you can't do an experiment.

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:57.630
I can't say, "Hmm,
Dave, what if the cosmos

00:27:57.630 --> 00:27:58.920
had twice as much dark matter?

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:00.250
Oh, let me just, I'm God,"

00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:01.720
and no, no, you can't do that.

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:04.750
Whereas you can have a
control and a variable

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:05.770
and do stuff in biology.

00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:08.830
So astronomy and in particular
cosmology is very hard.

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:11.100
But what you can do is take
a lot of artistic license.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:13.090
The fact that you can't
go near a black hole,

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:14.530
you can't go back to the Big Bang,

00:28:14.530 --> 00:28:16.540
means that you can do
a lot with simulations

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:19.163
that really let the imagination run wild.

00:28:20.059 --> 00:28:21.536
- I think that was your way of saying

00:28:21.536 --> 00:28:23.815
that the "Interstellar"
scene was pretty cool

00:28:23.815 --> 00:28:24.770
as he was going through things.

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:25.801
- It was.

00:28:25.801 --> 00:28:27.301
What happened, I never saw it.

00:28:28.138 --> 00:28:29.410
I had to go to the Mexican border

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:32.130
to get like a global entry
card a few years back.

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:33.620
I hadn't seen "Interstellar,"

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:37.030
and the guy in front of
me was a professor also

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:38.830
at Scripps Institution of Oceanography.

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:40.460
And I was like, "Oh,
crap, they're gonna think

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:41.460
like it's a conspiracy.

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:43.060
All these professors
are coming to the border

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:44.870
to get their passport or
to get their whatever."

00:28:44.870 --> 00:28:46.330
And the guy said to me,

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:47.750
first, I heard him ask the guy

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:49.907
in front of me some questions,
then he said to me like,

00:28:49.907 --> 00:28:52.770
"Um, well, oh, you're an astrophysicist.

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:54.050
You think you're smarter than me?

00:28:54.050 --> 00:28:55.470
You got a pretty big gun over there.

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:56.880
I better be careful."

00:28:56.880 --> 00:28:58.980
And I said, well, I hadn't seen it, Dave,

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:01.457
so I had to think fast
on my feet, and I said,

00:29:01.457 --> 00:29:04.300
"Well, um, Interstellar, hmm."

00:29:04.300 --> 00:29:06.000
He asked me, "What do you
think about 'Interstellar?'

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:06.833
You're so smart."

00:29:06.833 --> 00:29:09.040
"Well, the gravitational
spacetime continuum can be warped

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:11.766
by four dimensions, but
not in a manifestation."

00:29:11.766 --> 00:29:12.740
And I just BS'd my way out of it.

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:14.660
But luckily, I only spent three nights

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:16.500
in the Tijuana prison.

00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:18.410
- I've been doing that my
whole life with "Star Wars,"

00:29:18.410 --> 00:29:20.150
so you're good to go.

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:22.960
Duncan Haldane, the Alchemist.

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:26.440
- Yeah, so this is a guy
who along with colleagues

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:29.700
invented a theoretical type of matter,

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:32.720
a phase of matter not known to exist

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:34.873
and purely intellectually.

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:38.650
It's so weird, Dave, it's actually...

00:29:38.650 --> 00:29:39.960
We exist in three dimensions.

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:43.510
We can go up, down, backwards,
forwards, left, right.

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:46.640
But he envisioned a two-dimensional world,

00:29:46.640 --> 00:29:48.380
very different with time proceeding,

00:29:48.380 --> 00:29:50.930
but also confined to two dimensions.

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:55.610
Like an ant would feel
bulbous and curvy, right?

00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:56.600
But a flat surface.

00:29:56.600 --> 00:29:57.896
And then he asked,

00:29:57.896 --> 00:29:59.330
"Well, what would matter
behave like then?"

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:00.810
And he speculated,

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:03.787
and he came up with
this new type of matter

00:30:03.787 --> 00:30:04.860
and it would have certain properties

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:07.200
and would behave in an interesting way.

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:09.300
But everyone thought it
was purely mathematical,

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:12.140
until experimental physicists devised ways

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:15.650
to actually simulate a
two-dimensional world

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:17.880
by basically printing

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:21.500
with very special media types of exotic...

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:24.650
And then they found that these
objects behaved differently

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:26.390
in two dimensions than three dimensions.

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:30.370
So he really conceived in
his mind of a new element,

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:33.540
of a new type of matter, and
I found that very interesting.

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:35.260
And so I call him the Alchemist, yeah,

00:30:35.260 --> 00:30:36.470
so he was very clever.

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:37.550
Alchemists, of course, were people

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:41.650
that thought they could turn
ordinary base metals into gold,

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:43.330
and actually some of the
greatest minds in history

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:44.880
like Isaac Newton did that.

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:46.420
And that's actually part of what you said

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:50.070
in the very beginning, because
actually the hard sciences,

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:53.290
they have pretty shaky past histories.

00:30:53.290 --> 00:30:57.530
Chemistry evolved
essentially from alchemy.

00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:00.053
Astronomy evolved from astrology.

00:31:02.462 --> 00:31:05.480
And obviously, medicine
evolved from leeches

00:31:05.480 --> 00:31:08.500
and bloodletting like
we talked about earlier.

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:11.420
I think we have a tendency
to want to kind of glorify,

00:31:11.420 --> 00:31:13.936
oh, the scientific method.

00:31:13.936 --> 00:31:16.670
And there is no scientific
method, that's full stop.

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:20.060
There are ways to methodologically
approach scientific

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:21.890
or knowledge-based questions.

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:25.440
And I think this way of coming
up with a new form of matter

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:26.940
purely in the human brain,

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:28.540
I think that's just so fascinating.

00:31:28.540 --> 00:31:30.240
- How long did it take
before he met the guy

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:32.650
that was able to actually
replicate it in the real world?

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:34.470
- Years, years, and it's funny

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:35.850
because in the interview at the end,

00:31:35.850 --> 00:31:36.683
he starts talking about,

00:31:36.683 --> 00:31:39.230
"Well, I don't think this
is gonna make a new iPhone.

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:40.770
It's not gonna improve your life."

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:43.720
But I was like, the history
of science tells us otherwise,

00:31:43.720 --> 00:31:45.560
like there's a very good chance.

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:46.890
If you ask these guys who discovered

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:49.120
this hiss of microwaves back in the '60s,

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:51.290
would we be able to watch?

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:53.610
I always say my grandmother, Dave,

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:57.450
she grew up in the (indistinct)
seeing horse and buggies,

00:31:57.450 --> 00:32:00.690
and she grew up to see human beings, men,

00:32:00.690 --> 00:32:03.119
walking on the surface of TikTok.

00:32:03.119 --> 00:32:03.952
(Dave laughing)

00:32:03.952 --> 00:32:04.785
And how wonderful is that?

00:32:04.785 --> 00:32:06.660
So I'm like telling this guy, Haldane,

00:32:07.982 --> 00:32:10.332
"Don't underestimate what
the human mind can do

00:32:11.318 --> 00:32:12.151
with enough time."

00:32:12.151 --> 00:32:14.823
- Frank Wilczek a beautiful mind.

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:19.900
- Yeah, Frank Wilczek, he
is a professor at MIT now.

00:32:19.900 --> 00:32:22.510
And when he was in his early 20s, Dave,

00:32:22.510 --> 00:32:24.850
he came up with an idea so brilliant

00:32:24.850 --> 00:32:26.370
along with his colleagues,

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:28.680
that it was guaranteed
to win a Nobel Prize.

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:33.680
In 1974, he was told he is
the wunderkind of science.

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:37.470
He is going to guarantee to
make a trip to Stockholm.

00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:38.660
There's just one catch,

00:32:38.660 --> 00:32:41.337
there's about 20 other
guys in front of you

00:32:41.337 --> 00:32:43.980
that have to get their Nobel Prizes almost

00:32:43.980 --> 00:32:47.400
in order for you to have the
validation and the verification

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:50.680
that is necessary in the scientific method

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:53.800
to get consensus around this physics idea.

00:32:53.800 --> 00:32:56.157
Now, Dave, I imagine, if I told you.

00:32:56.157 --> 00:32:59.430
"You're gonna win the lottery
next week, next year,"

00:32:59.430 --> 00:33:01.220
you'd be pretty happy.

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:03.700
If I told you we're
gonna win it in 30 years,

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:05.300
what would go through your mind?

00:33:06.447 --> 00:33:07.767
I think that I'd be thinking about,

00:33:07.767 --> 00:33:09.330
"Hm, I gotta stay alive.

00:33:09.330 --> 00:33:11.407
I gotta cut back on the burgers."

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:15.670
But imagine that terror and the pressure,

00:33:15.670 --> 00:33:20.310
and so many of them, I think,
there was such a long delay

00:33:20.310 --> 00:33:24.200
between the work that won
them the prize eventually,

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:25.880
and the actual awarding of the prize

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:28.700
on December 10th every
year in Stockholm, Sweden,

00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:30.660
and I think that that is a lesson

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:31.990
in resiliency and perseverance

00:33:31.990 --> 00:33:34.400
because he also didn't rest on that.

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:36.240
I might just say, "Okay, cool.

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:38.800
Put me in a coma, put on the feeding tube,

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:40.850
this Woody Allen in "Sleeper" outfit,

00:33:40.850 --> 00:33:42.760
and wake me up when Stockholm.

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:43.593
But no, he didn't.

00:33:43.593 --> 00:33:46.630
He went on to invent or
create many, many new things.

00:33:46.630 --> 00:33:49.840
And he's an inspiration
in many ways from that.

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:50.740
He's also a great writer.

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:52.440
He writes for "The Wall Street Journal,"

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:54.030
a science column for "The
Wall Street Journal,"

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:56.330
which is, you know, it's
not technical at all.

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:59.130
He's an incredible intellect.

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:00.380
- Speaking of Woody Allen and "Sleeper,"

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:02.540
do we still not have
the cryo sleep things?

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:04.010
I mean, they were in "Interstellar," too.

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:05.200
They're in all the alien movies.

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:06.230
Do those things exist?

00:34:06.230 --> 00:34:08.270
Where are we at with those things?

00:34:08.270 --> 00:34:09.130
- I don't know.

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:12.410
Every time I go down to
my biology lab colleagues,

00:34:12.410 --> 00:34:15.543
they're always asleep or dissecting frogs.

00:34:16.718 --> 00:34:19.096
If you had access to those
many chemicals and drugs, Dave,

00:34:19.096 --> 00:34:19.929
what would you be doing, come on?

00:34:19.929 --> 00:34:22.520
- You would put yourself in a
bag in some hot water usually,

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:23.600
and then they get you.

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:24.720
It was in "The Martian," too,

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:26.167
with what's his name.
- You gonna put them

00:34:26.167 --> 00:34:27.140
in one of those brownies

00:34:27.140 --> 00:34:28.270
that you still have leftover from 1997.

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:29.840
- Exactly, I wish had those.

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:32.550
John Mather, the Collaborator.

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:34.720
- Yeah, John is an incredible guy

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:37.000
who also is kind of a wunderkind

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:38.880
and did a tremendous amount of work.

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:41.610
He is the stereotypical,
although he's not a professor,

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:46.420
he's a stereotypical
professor, just super smart,

00:34:46.420 --> 00:34:49.920
can answer and knows
everything about the field

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:53.170
and has contributed to many,
many great discoveries,

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:56.570
and is about to be the chief scientist

00:34:56.570 --> 00:34:59.030
behind this new space telescope

00:34:59.030 --> 00:35:01.280
that's gonna be launched
in December, hopefully,

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:03.100
after a 20-year delay.

00:35:03.100 --> 00:35:05.280
It's one of the most expensive
projects NASA's ever done.

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:08.330
And it's a successor to
the Hubble Space Telescope,

00:35:08.330 --> 00:35:11.260
which has revealed more wonders
and mysteries of the cosmos

00:35:11.260 --> 00:35:12.730
than any other project.

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:17.070
And so he discovered not serendipitously,

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:18.660
so he's an example of a different type

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:20.250
of scientific discovery,

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:23.720
which is that you make an
extremely precise measurement

00:35:23.720 --> 00:35:26.390
of a known quantity, something
that we knew existed,

00:35:26.390 --> 00:35:28.890
this microwave background
radiation that I study.

00:35:28.890 --> 00:35:29.950
Comes in all directions,

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:32.420
originally discovered in New Jersey.

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:34.630
But he made the most
exquisite measurement ever

00:35:34.630 --> 00:35:38.440
that put the nail in the coffin
of any other alternatives

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:40.620
that could plausibly be
said to be responsible

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:42.820
for the appearance of our universe.

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:46.200
Namely, there are people
that believed up until then

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:47.850
in the early '90s,

00:35:47.850 --> 00:35:49.630
that the universe could
have existed forever.

00:35:49.630 --> 00:35:51.560
in what's called the steady-state.

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:53.970
And the measurements that
he did for technical reasons

00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:56.030
really destroyed all hope

00:35:56.030 --> 00:35:58.690
for those who believed in an alternative

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:02.860
to what we now call the Big
Bang initiation of the universe.

00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:07.500
So he is this really
quintessential workman-like,

00:36:07.500 --> 00:36:11.430
studious, bookish, very, very
bright, very, very diligent.

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:12.920
He just keeps going.

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:16.000
Again, these guys could all
retire and be super famous

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:17.710
and do whatever they wanna do.

00:36:17.710 --> 00:36:20.265
And yet he's working super hard

00:36:20.265 --> 00:36:22.530
on this next great space telescope,

00:36:22.530 --> 00:36:25.500
the dividends of which may
not come for decades or more.

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:26.940
- How does it go for a guy like that

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:30.090
when he sort of puts that
final nail as you put it

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:33.140
and it sort of blows up
what many in his field

00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:34.020
think about something?

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:36.370
I suspect there's probably
a lot of resentment.

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:37.800
There's probably a lot of denial.

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:41.580
But slowly kind of people
come along usually?

00:36:41.580 --> 00:36:43.820
- Yeah, well, that's what's so
interesting in that chapter.

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:46.670
He talks about the importance
of listening to your critics,

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:48.310
but not too much.

00:36:48.310 --> 00:36:52.360
And I kinda summarize this
as a trait in my students.

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:54.580
I say you have to be humble.

00:36:54.580 --> 00:36:56.040
Nobody likes someone who's not humble.

00:36:56.040 --> 00:36:58.520
And we'll talk about
imposter syndrome and stuff

00:36:58.520 --> 00:36:59.600
in a little bit, I'm sure.

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:02.670
But you need humility,
but you need swagger, too.

00:37:02.670 --> 00:37:04.940
You need the swagger to know
that you're doing a good job,

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:06.420
that you are competent.

00:37:06.420 --> 00:37:08.510
You can't just go, "Oh,
I'm timid, I'm humble,

00:37:08.510 --> 00:37:09.840
I can't do anything."

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:13.851
And in his case, there were
these really vociferous calls

00:37:13.851 --> 00:37:15.550
that the Big Bang is wrong

00:37:15.550 --> 00:37:19.660
and it's actually just a product
of the religious scientist,

00:37:19.660 --> 00:37:21.260
if you can believe such a thing,

00:37:22.147 --> 00:37:24.700
and this impulse that human beings have

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:27.390
to believe in Genesis
1:1, et cetera, et cetera.

00:37:27.390 --> 00:37:31.020
And in the end, he said, "You
can listen to those people.

00:37:31.020 --> 00:37:33.830
You can respect their
scientific contributions.

00:37:33.830 --> 00:37:35.540
But you shouldn't listen so much

00:37:35.540 --> 00:37:38.670
that you really undermine
your own confidence."

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:40.770
So it's listening to your critics,

00:37:40.770 --> 00:37:45.293
not necessarily letting their
comments go to your heart,

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:49.620
but maybe ingesting them into your mind,

00:37:49.620 --> 00:37:52.680
processing them analytically,
and saying, "Is there a way?"

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:55.470
And I translated this and
we talked about this before.

00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:57.540
Can you steelman your critic?

00:37:57.540 --> 00:37:59.920
And can you build up his
or her counterargument

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:02.040
to your pet theory or pet experiment?

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:05.290
And if you can't, it's not a
guarantee that you're wrong.

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:07.420
You may be right and they may
be just a blundering idiot

00:38:07.420 --> 00:38:09.410
who just believes in some stupid model.

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:11.220
But in the case where you can actually

00:38:11.220 --> 00:38:12.600
reproduce their arguments,

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:15.230
it makes your case that much more strong.

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:18.800
And I think that's a trait I
want to imbue in other people

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:20.410
to take to heart no
matter what their field,

00:38:20.410 --> 00:38:22.070
and I think he exemplifies that.

00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:25.050
- I suspect that goes far
better in the laboratories

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:28.013
that you people work in
rather than the Twitterscape.

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:31.860
I'm pretty sure I don't need
to run an experiment on that.

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:34.780
My life has been a living
experiment of that.

00:38:34.780 --> 00:38:36.500
We've got one more, and
then if you don't mind,

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:38.610
I'm going to throw in my own scientist.

00:38:38.610 --> 00:38:40.720
I have not planned anything
in advance with you,

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:41.730
but I'm gonna throw in someone

00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:43.224
that has influenced me tremendously.

00:38:43.224 --> 00:38:44.057
- Absolutely.

00:38:44.057 --> 00:38:44.980
- But the last one, number nine,

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:49.630
Barry Barish, the Avuncular Avatar.

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:52.740
- Yeah, so Barry kind of
represents the physicist

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:56.300
that I wanna be and in
the avatar aspect of that.

00:38:56.300 --> 00:38:58.820
And avuncular 'cause
he is just such a warm,

00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:00.860
gracious individual.

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:03.290
So Barry really in some
ways is responsible

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:04.540
for this book's existence

00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:07.620
because it was during the
recording of this episode

00:39:07.620 --> 00:39:10.510
with him on my podcast,
"Into the "Impossible,"

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:13.450
that he said something that
was kind of interesting

00:39:13.450 --> 00:39:14.440
and provocative to me.

00:39:14.440 --> 00:39:17.640
And then I went further,
learning the Rubinic skills

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:20.180
that I've learned from
you, and I went deeper.

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:21.013
What are you talking about?

00:39:21.013 --> 00:39:23.000
He said to me, Dave, and it blew me away,

00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:24.630
I said, "When you were young,

00:39:24.630 --> 00:39:26.930
were you like this stereotypical
brilliant kid in math?"

00:39:26.930 --> 00:39:29.140
And he's like, "No, I
was never, I was good,

00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:30.780
I was a hard worker, I was smart."

00:39:30.780 --> 00:39:33.018
But he grew up in Oklahoma

00:39:33.018 --> 00:39:35.870
and then later moved to LA and so forth.

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:37.310
And then he was just like persistent

00:39:37.310 --> 00:39:38.840
and he always wanted to
do a certain type of work

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:40.040
called particle physics.

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:43.760
But he was never under this
impression that he was great.

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:46.710
And I said, "Did you ever
have the imposter syndrome

00:39:46.710 --> 00:39:47.820
when you were at UC Berkeley

00:39:47.820 --> 00:39:51.030
working for these famous
Nobel laureate physicists?

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:52.630
Did you ever feel like
you weren't good enough?"

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:54.930
He goes, "What do you mean, did I?

00:39:54.930 --> 00:39:57.400
I still feel the imposter syndrome."

00:39:57.400 --> 00:39:58.940
I was like, "What are you talking about?"

00:39:58.940 --> 00:40:01.740
He said, "I feel it worse than ever."

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:05.307
And I said, "You gotta explain,
what are you talking about?"

00:40:05.307 --> 00:40:07.850
And he said, "When you
win the Nobel prize,

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:11.270
you go to Stockholm, you
meet the king, you bend down,

00:40:11.270 --> 00:40:14.840
and he puts this golden
Gildan engraved image

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:16.730
of Alfred Nobel on your neck,

00:40:16.730 --> 00:40:18.470
and you get a check for some fraction

00:40:18.470 --> 00:40:21.603
or 100% of a million dollar payout,

00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:24.660
and you get this portrait,
this really beautiful portrait.

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:26.020
I tried to reproduce the portrait

00:40:26.020 --> 00:40:28.570
with a really nice work
of an artist in this book.

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:30.120
I think he did a wonderful job.

00:40:31.838 --> 00:40:34.077
And then you have to sign
this ledger that says,

00:40:34.077 --> 00:40:36.380
"Yes, I received my Nobel Prize

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:39.707
and all the accoutrements
and delicacies therein."

00:40:40.957 --> 00:40:43.810
And he said, "When I did that,
I'm a very curious person,

00:40:43.810 --> 00:40:45.970
I couldn't resist who
signed it before me."

00:40:45.970 --> 00:40:47.100
So he turned back the pages.

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:50.788
He saw Richard Feynman. Marie Curie,

00:40:50.788 --> 00:40:52.520
and he saw Albert Einstein.

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:54.947
And he said, Dave, and
the hair still stands up,

00:40:54.947 --> 00:40:56.370
and he said, "I'm not worthy.

00:40:56.370 --> 00:40:58.260
I am not Einstein.

00:40:58.260 --> 00:40:59.790
I am not good enough to be there.

00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:03.200
And it made me feel completely inadequate,

00:41:03.200 --> 00:41:05.313
completely not up to the task.

00:41:06.943 --> 00:41:08.330
And now I'm in the same
league as Einstein.

00:41:08.330 --> 00:41:10.190
I do not deserve it."

00:41:10.190 --> 00:41:13.800
And I said, thinking quickly,
I said, "Barry, guess what?

00:41:13.800 --> 00:41:15.890
You know who also had
the imposter syndrome?

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:17.240
Albert Einstein.

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:19.890
Because Albert Einstein was in awe

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:23.217
and wholly inadequate
before Isaac Newton."

00:41:24.510 --> 00:41:26.150
And he felt Isaac Newton, Dave,

00:41:26.150 --> 00:41:28.180
can you imagine if Einstein
were tweeting today?

00:41:28.180 --> 00:41:29.013
He said the following.

00:41:29.013 --> 00:41:31.990
He said, "Isaac Newton
revolutionized not only science,

00:41:31.990 --> 00:41:34.930
but Western culture,
Western civilization."

00:41:34.930 --> 00:41:36.270
He'd get canceled immediately.

00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:40.200
He said, "Newton had more
influence on civilization,

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:43.680
not just science, than any
human before or since,"

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:46.090
meaning including Albert Einstein

00:41:46.090 --> 00:41:47.870
who was writing this about Newton.

00:41:47.870 --> 00:41:51.030
And I said, "But wait,
Barry, it doesn't stop there.

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:55.432
Isaac Newton had the severe
case of the imposter syndrome."

00:41:55.432 --> 00:41:56.265
He said, "What do you mean?"

00:41:56.265 --> 00:41:59.320
I said, "He felt completely
inadequate before his idol,

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:02.180
not a scientist, but Jesus Christ."

00:42:02.180 --> 00:42:05.430
In fact, Newton said his
highest accomplishment in life

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:08.130
is that he died a virgin,
like Jesus Christ,

00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:10.440
and he wanted to emulate
Christ in every way.

00:42:10.440 --> 00:42:12.130
So, I think it's very fascinating.

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:13.210
And guess what?

00:42:13.210 --> 00:42:15.947
I don't know, (laughing)

00:42:15.947 --> 00:42:17.250
maybe Jesus didn't feel
the imposter syndrome,

00:42:17.250 --> 00:42:18.440
but he was pretty humble

00:42:18.440 --> 00:42:21.900
and I think he was a pretty
brilliant human being,

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:24.360
or God, depending on
your perspective, right?

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:27.320
So, I think we all can learn this lesson

00:42:27.320 --> 00:42:30.370
because if you think that
you're the greatest person

00:42:30.370 --> 00:42:32.270
and you have these great contributions

00:42:32.270 --> 00:42:33.790
and you have like, "I
won the Nobel prize,"

00:42:33.790 --> 00:42:35.330
like there are guys,
I'm not gonna say who,

00:42:35.330 --> 00:42:36.767
but there are people in the book who said,

00:42:36.767 --> 00:42:37.980
"I don't have the imposter syndrome,

00:42:37.980 --> 00:42:39.470
I deserve this fricking thing,"

00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:43.040
like Flavor Flav with the huge
medallion around his neck.

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:45.990
But they feel like they deserve
it, but not all of them.

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:48.380
And I think that it was a dichotomy

00:42:48.380 --> 00:42:49.960
that I wasn't suspecting.

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:52.470
It was a dichotomy that you need to have,

00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:55.199
like I said, humility and swagger,

00:42:55.199 --> 00:42:57.510
but you need to be very careful about it

00:42:57.510 --> 00:43:01.570
because the imposter syndrome
is born out of insecurity,

00:43:01.570 --> 00:43:04.930
and people that are
arrogant and unpleasant,

00:43:04.930 --> 00:43:06.690
they're also insecure, right?
- Mm-mm.

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:09.490
The old joke, and you
know this from your dog,

00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:11.330
the smallest dog barks the loudest, right?

00:43:11.330 --> 00:43:12.400
That's the thing, right?

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:13.920
You talked to Jocko on your podcast.

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:15.650
Jocko's not like, ""Oh,
I'm gonna kick your..."

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:18.300
He's just like, "Whatever,
man, I'll run away.

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:21.270
I'll put on my running
shoes and get out of there."

00:43:21.270 --> 00:43:23.410
So, I think that's really interesting

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:25.810
'cause it's very rare that the opposite,

00:43:25.810 --> 00:43:28.910
two different traits
in diametric opposition

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:29.990
come from the same source.

00:43:29.990 --> 00:43:32.480
Insecurity is responsible
for imposter syndrome

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:36.760
and for the arrogance that
many non-humble people feel.

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:39.110
So, I felt it was
important to distill this,

00:43:39.110 --> 00:43:40.700
and who better to learn it from

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:41.930
than someone who's achieved

00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:44.820
and gotten into the
promised land of my field

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:45.670
is the Nobel Prize.

00:43:45.670 --> 00:43:48.220
There's no higher thing in all of science,

00:43:48.220 --> 00:43:51.130
maybe even all of culture,
civilization, and society

00:43:51.130 --> 00:43:52.120
than the Nobel Prize.

00:43:52.120 --> 00:43:54.670
And so I felt these people,
and in particular Barry,

00:43:54.670 --> 00:43:55.503
and I was so honored

00:43:55.503 --> 00:43:58.300
that he wrote the foreword
to the book as well.

00:43:58.300 --> 00:44:02.740
That was really a great honor
that I do not feel worthy of.

00:44:02.740 --> 00:44:04.890
- Brian, that was a beautiful ending

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:07.320
to the interview in a normal situation.

00:44:07.320 --> 00:44:11.430
But I feel that I must ask
you about one other scientist

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:15.750
who was hugely influential
I believe at Queens College,

00:44:15.750 --> 00:44:18.830
and I think maybe he
was a nuclear scientist,

00:44:18.830 --> 00:44:21.721
Dr. Otto Octavius.

00:44:21.721 --> 00:44:22.554
Do you know of him?

00:44:22.554 --> 00:44:25.660
- (laughing) Octavius, no, I
do not know of him personally.

00:44:25.660 --> 00:44:27.770
Is he a Nobel caliber scientist?

00:44:27.770 --> 00:44:29.870
- Doc Ock from "Spider-Man."

00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:30.880
Doc Ock.
- Octopus.

00:44:30.880 --> 00:44:33.460
- He was doing something with
nuclear power with the arms,

00:44:33.460 --> 00:44:35.810
and then he got them fused to his back,

00:44:35.810 --> 00:44:37.470
and then the little
inhibitor chip blew up.

00:44:37.470 --> 00:44:40.536
Next thing you know, he's
working with Mysterio

00:44:40.536 --> 00:44:41.460
and the rest of the guys.

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:43.200
- Spider-Man was very prescient

00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:45.760
because he also pre-staged the
existence of the multiverse

00:44:45.760 --> 00:44:47.833
and the Spider-Verse, right?

00:44:49.199 --> 00:44:50.460
- And now we're all
entering the multiverse,

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:51.420
that's the new thing.

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:52.520
There's this rumor going around now

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:54.540
that Facebook's actually
gonna rename itself

00:44:54.540 --> 00:44:55.460
the multiverse or something?

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:56.956
Have you heard about this?

00:44:56.956 --> 00:44:58.810
- (laughing) No, I have
not heard about that.

00:44:58.810 --> 00:45:01.100
Yeah, no, that makes me
want to take a sabbath

00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:03.990
from all social media networks,

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:06.230
except to subscribe to your feed.

00:45:06.230 --> 00:45:07.330
- I've thoroughly enjoyed this.

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:08.720
Actually, I have one more for you,

00:45:08.720 --> 00:45:13.400
which is, are we living in a simulation?

00:45:13.400 --> 00:45:14.380
- Ah, this is a fascinating-

00:45:14.380 --> 00:45:16.133
- How's that for a final question?

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:19.023
- Well, usually, Dave,
people have preface it,

00:45:19.023 --> 00:45:20.406
"I have a simple question,

00:45:20.406 --> 00:45:23.040
like how do you reconcile believing in God

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:25.666
with the existence of the Big Bang?"

00:45:25.666 --> 00:45:27.810
And there, that would be a
simple question, the simulation.

00:45:27.810 --> 00:45:29.640
So what's so interesting
about the simulation

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:32.880
is that it actually has a lot of bases

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:35.370
in both classical philosophy,

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:37.200
kind of the Pascal's wager,

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:42.052
kind of the Descartean, cogito, ergo sum,

00:45:42.052 --> 00:45:42.885
I think therefore I am.

00:45:42.885 --> 00:45:45.140
People don't realize
that that was predicated.

00:45:45.140 --> 00:45:46.990
So Descartes said, "How do I know

00:45:46.990 --> 00:45:48.490
I'm not just a brain in a vat?"

00:45:48.490 --> 00:45:51.350
Basically, that was their
simulation of the time.

00:45:51.350 --> 00:45:53.280
And he said, "I know I am not

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:55.700
because I think, therefore I am."

00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:57.360
But there was a predicate sentence,

00:45:57.360 --> 00:45:59.460
which was that God created him.

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:01.090
So in other words, actually,

00:46:01.090 --> 00:46:03.150
Descartes can't answer
that question without God.

00:46:03.150 --> 00:46:05.110
And it's funny because
the simulation hypothesis,

00:46:05.110 --> 00:46:06.880
for the listeners that may not know,

00:46:06.880 --> 00:46:08.480
is basically the supposition

00:46:08.480 --> 00:46:12.380
that as computing power doubles
every year, year and a half,

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:14.320
according to what's known as Moore's law,

00:46:14.320 --> 00:46:16.900
your computers get cheaper,
they get more powerful,

00:46:16.900 --> 00:46:20.890
they get faster, and
that that extrapolation,

00:46:20.890 --> 00:46:22.340
there's no natural starting point,

00:46:22.340 --> 00:46:24.280
I mean, when you made the
first computer or whatever

00:46:24.280 --> 00:46:25.410
that for us is.

00:46:25.410 --> 00:46:28.420
But could it not be that
they get to so much power,

00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:31.580
that they can simulate
every single physical object

00:46:31.580 --> 00:46:33.800
in the universe and they can simulate,

00:46:33.800 --> 00:46:35.560
and if we're just material objects,

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:38.250
our brains are just wet computers,

00:46:38.250 --> 00:46:39.200
but they're just computers,

00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:42.270
they have neurons, they have
carbon, and DNA and so forth,

00:46:42.270 --> 00:46:45.080
but why couldn't that be simulated

00:46:45.080 --> 00:46:48.200
in some great, vast
supercomputer of the future

00:46:48.200 --> 00:46:49.690
that doesn't exist yet?

00:46:49.690 --> 00:46:53.430
But we would be kind of like
the "SimCity" or whatever.

00:46:53.430 --> 00:46:54.910
- Right, we'd be in
"The Matrix" in essence.

00:46:54.910 --> 00:46:56.670
- Yeah, we'd be in "The Matrix."

00:46:56.670 --> 00:47:00.930
So, there are a lot of ways
to actually test for that

00:47:00.930 --> 00:47:02.370
and actually probe for it.

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:04.380
There's no evidence that
we are in a simulation,

00:47:04.380 --> 00:47:06.180
that we do live in a matrix.

00:47:06.180 --> 00:47:09.570
For one question, it could
have this infinite regress

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:10.410
of possibilities.

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:12.610
So you could say to me,
"We are in a simulation."

00:47:12.610 --> 00:47:15.440
I could say, "Well, whoever
made that simulation,

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:16.710
they're a simulation,

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:17.837
and whoever made that simulation..."

00:47:17.837 --> 00:47:19.750
And so you would have to have an infinite,

00:47:19.750 --> 00:47:21.650
not just a large amount of computer,

00:47:21.650 --> 00:47:24.100
you need an infinite
amount of computing power,

00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:26.250
and there would be certain glitches.

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:27.660
The first thing I would want to know is,

00:47:27.660 --> 00:47:29.800
why are there so many damn Kardashians?

00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:33.480
Why do we need so many
Kardashians in this simulation?

00:47:33.480 --> 00:47:35.350
But I think it stems from

00:47:35.350 --> 00:47:38.300
both extrapolating the computing power,

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:40.260
which actually isn't growing,

00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:43.070
in terms of like the number of
transistors, that's growing,

00:47:43.070 --> 00:47:44.760
but because the computers
get better and better,

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:46.310
more people wanna use them, Dave,

00:47:46.310 --> 00:47:47.800
and actually they start to slow down

00:47:47.800 --> 00:47:49.380
because their demands are so much higher.

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:50.970
They're victims of their own success.

00:47:50.970 --> 00:47:52.610
And there's no reason to
think that won't happen

00:47:52.610 --> 00:47:55.010
either with quantum computers or whatever.

00:47:55.010 --> 00:47:57.730
And the last thing I wanna say is that,

00:47:57.730 --> 00:47:59.970
imagine if there is a simulation

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:02.150
and there is some simulator,

00:48:02.150 --> 00:48:04.720
and let's say it's not
infinite, there's just one,

00:48:04.720 --> 00:48:09.290
what ethical responsibilities
does that simulation have

00:48:10.570 --> 00:48:12.370
to the things that it's simulating?

00:48:12.370 --> 00:48:13.593
'Cause we feel pain.

00:48:14.450 --> 00:48:17.650
You could imagine we can create
artificial intelligence, AI,

00:48:17.650 --> 00:48:19.240
and that could feel pain.

00:48:19.240 --> 00:48:20.073
You could make it

00:48:20.073 --> 00:48:21.220
so that if it does
something you don't like,

00:48:21.220 --> 00:48:23.490
you unplug it, or you pop a transistor,

00:48:23.490 --> 00:48:27.710
or you nail a capacitor in the
middle of the circuit board

00:48:27.710 --> 00:48:29.130
and it could feel pain.

00:48:29.130 --> 00:48:32.243
But can it feel the kinds of inspiration,

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:34.070
the ghost in the machine?

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:36.480
We don't know yet if that's possible.

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:37.620
I happen to be a pessimist.

00:48:37.620 --> 00:48:39.350
I don't think that artificial intelligence

00:48:39.350 --> 00:48:40.860
will take over the universe

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:44.210
or even create new physics or
new art or things like that.

00:48:44.210 --> 00:48:46.440
That's categorically different
from what we're seeing now.

00:48:46.440 --> 00:48:48.080
In other words, I like to say,

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:51.030
humans can be beaten
by computers at chess,

00:48:51.030 --> 00:48:54.310
but can a computer invent a game of chess?

00:48:54.310 --> 00:48:55.160
I don't think so.

00:48:56.010 --> 00:49:00.610
- Brian, you are an excellent
science communicator.

00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:01.760
I've thoroughly enjoyed this.

00:49:01.760 --> 00:49:02.960
It was a little different

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:05.420
from our day-to-day political lunacy,

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:07.640
but I suspect my audience
will enjoy it as well.

00:49:07.640 --> 00:49:10.230
We are gonna link to the
book right down below,

00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:12.320
and I've enjoyed this.

00:49:12.320 --> 00:49:14.490
But for the record, not
a huge fan of your boss,

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:16.720
Gavin Newsom, just in
case anyone was unclear

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:17.850
on their feelings on that.

00:49:17.850 --> 00:49:20.770
- Well, we'll see if you
get tenure after that.

00:49:20.770 --> 00:49:21.603
- Thanks, Brian.

